Say where a living process is stuck.

The display path is now complete on the kernel side and the window server
agrees. backboardd opens the framebuffer, runs the whole mode set --
selectors 18, 57, 7, 15, 22, 8, 33, 74 -- registers both notification
types, takes the frame clock, and turns hardware vsync on:

    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] notifications on for type 5
    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 72 in 4{9018240232,5268125696,5,0}

and then nothing, for as long as the handset is left on. No selector 3, no
swap, and -- the part that says where the fault really is -- not one
IOCoreSurfaceRoot open, by any process, ever. On a live system dozens of
processes hold surfaces by this point. Here nobody has asked for one.

Three things were tried against the window server and all three landed
without changing that. CA::WindowServer::IOMFBServer::add_vsync_source
ends with a gate on a client count that is zero, so hardware vsync was
never switched on; removing the gate switched it on, and reading
vsync_callback afterwards showed why it changed nothing -- the count is a
one-shot render request that the callback itself decrements, so forcing
the clock on produces a clock with nothing to drive. Then the display
itself: CA::WindowServer::IOMFBDisplay::can_update() calls is_enabled()
through the vtable and returns false the moment it is false, and
AppleDisplay's constructor builds its flag word out of 0x9004, which does
not include the enabled bit, while the only thing that sets that bit is
-[CAWindowServerDisplay setDisabled:] reached from a MIG message nothing
here sends. Making the display be born enabled, and then making
is_enabled() unconditional, changed nothing either.

That exhausts what can be learned by reading the window server. Everything
it is supposed to do, it has done; what is missing is a client. SpringBoard
is alive in every roll call, at the same pid, for many minutes, and it
never crashes and never allocates a surface, so it is not failing -- it is
waiting. backboardd may be waiting too: if its main thread is still inside
-[CAWindowServer _detectDisplays] then the Mach services it is supposed to
register do not exist yet, and SpringBoard blocking on a bootstrap lookup
of one of them would look exactly like this from outside.

A wait leaves no corpse, so exitbt has nothing to print. But a blocked
thread keeps its user state in the same place a dying one does, and
backtrace_thread_user() in osfmk already knows how to walk another task's
stack: it takes a reference on the target's map, switches to it so copyin
resolves there, walks the frame chain and switches back. What is missing
is only the loop over a process's threads, which is the first half of this
change: proc_user_backtrace(pid) collects the threads under the proc lock,
references them so a libdispatch worker retiring mid-walk cannot be walked
after it is freed, drops the lock, and prints one line of return addresses
per thread.

The second half hangs it off the roll call, which already walks the pid
space and names what answers. btname is a comma separated list of name
prefixes -- p_comm is truncated to sixteen characters, so a prefix is all
there is to match against -- and it defaults to SpringBoard,backboardd,
because backtracing seventy idle processes would bury the two lines that
matter on a console with no scrollback. btname= with nothing after it
turns it off.

These are addresses and not symbols, for the same reason exitbt's are:
nothing in the kernel knows where any of it was loaded. That has been
enough every time so far. A slide is a multiple of 0x4000, so the low
fourteen bits of a frame name the call site whatever the slide is, and the
distance between two frames inside one image is invariant; a frame that
lands in a dylib already extracted from the shared cache pins the slide
outright, and then every other frame in that cache can be named.
